sources.what_it_proves:
Part I, labelled PROVED/EXACT. The method: a K-window subset automaton of the right half with a free cell K+1, transitions Z_u(S)=step({w∈S: w_1=u},0) at zero letters and O(S)=step(S,1) at one letters. Criterion: if every recurrent SCC of the period graph G_w has #edges=#vertices, the interface u* (column 1 at the zero-rows of w) is eventually periodic, hence column -1 is eventually periodic. Jen/Kopra then forbid word w as an eventual period for every number-like configuration.
Excluded words:
- 1^b0^a for b>=9, a>=1;
- 1^bv for b>=29 and |v|<=12;
- 0^m11 for m=6 and m>=8;
- every necklace whose cyclic zero-runs are all >=19 (family certificate cover14_19_48);
- every word with isolated 1s and zero-runs >=10 (quench lemma, three SAT facts);
- 1 0^9 (spine enumeration);
- 1^7 0 (K=10) and 0^7 1 (K=12);
- both constants.
Boundary: the method's reach is exactly the words whose fibre interface language L(w) has only eventually periodic members. If L(w) is uncountable the method fails at every K. For w=01, L(01) has measured positive entropy on random right halves, and uncountability is OPEN. A 20-cell trap block B=10011000100101000101 forces interface (10)^∞ under (01)-driving for any tail. Fibre surjectivity: every centre sequence c and right context r give exactly one configuration. Parts II-V cover a Duhamel/2-kernel representation, the diagonal defect front, and a window monoid containing A5 (relevant to P3).

