useful_theorems:
Rigorously excluded eventual period words (T-independent, all number-like configurations; finite parts independently re-verified by me):
- 0 1^q for q=7 and q>=9;
- 1 0^q for q>=10, plus 1 0^9 (Berzai, spine argument, not re-checked);
- 0^6 11, and 0^m 11 for m>=8;
- 1^b 0^a for b>=9, a>=1; 1^b v for b>=29, |v|<=12;
- 1 0^7 (K=12) and 1^7 0 (K=10);
- every word with isolated 1s and all zero-runs >= 10;
- every necklace with all cyclic zero-runs >= 19 (family certificate, not re-checked);
- 139 listed words of period 10..18 (placego entry 41).
My scan of all primitive words of period <= 7: none certified, at K=10 and by two-sided strips up to radius 7. Period 8: only 00000011 and 01111111 at K=10 (K=12 adds 00000001). Period 9: none at K <= 12. The brief's drivers 001, 0111, 0001, 0011 are uncertified at K=12.

Period-2 necessary conditions (rule30-foundry). With c(2k)=0 and c(2k+1)=1:
- x_{-1}=1 at odd times and x_{-1}(2k)=1-s_k, where s_k = x_1(2k);
- s_{k+1} = NOR(s,u,v) for the first three right cells, so the sampled word s avoids 11 and 00000 (exhaustively re-verified);
- left-only condition: G^k(n) mod 4 ∈ {1,2} for the integer-encoded left half n, with G = T_1∘T_0 and T_b(n) = (n>>1) xor (n|(n<<1)|b).

Dibujaron Lean (Mathlib), unconditional facts about the seed:
- white runs of c starting at a>=1 have length <= 3a; black runs have length < a;
- both colours occur in [a,4a]; at least n of each colour among the first 5^n bits;
- left-diagonal periods are unbounded, with some k <= 4^(2^a)+1 not 2^a-periodic;
- right-diagonal periods are unbounded;
- adjacent columns are never eventually complementary.

claims.statement:
Let c: N→Bool be eventually periodic, and let s be a left-half start (positions p ≤ -1) with finite support whose leftmost black cell is at -m (m ≥ 1). Let L be the left half driven by c:
- L(0,p) = s(p) for p ≤ -1;
- L(t,0) = c(t);
- L(t+1,p) = L(t,p-1) ⊕ (L(t,p) ∨ L(t,p+1)) for p ≤ -1.

Then for every q ≤ -1 the column t ↦ L(t,q) is NOT eventually periodic. In particular column -1 is never eventually periodic. So left-absorbing words do not exist, for any start state. Among states satisfying the forced constraints, column -1 is always aperiodic. The angle's alternative "column -1 is forced eventually periodic" is therefore vacuous: it can only mean that no state survives.

In the real pattern: if column i is periodic from T with period P, then column i+1 is not P-periodic from T on the times where column i is white.

For i = 0: suppose c is eventually periodic. Then the following three statements are equivalent, and all are false:
- column -1 is eventually periodic;
- orCol = c ∨ column 1 is eventually periodic;
- column 1 restricted to Z = {t : c(t) = 0} is eventually periodic, with a period that is also a period of c.

Consequence: the words for which column -1 is forced eventually periodic are exactly those for which the right half forces column 1 to be periodic on Z(w). A right-side absorption argument only needs column 1 to become periodic on Z(w), not everywhere.

If center(T+2k)=1 and center(T+2k+1)=0 for all k (every eventual period 2 has this form for some T), then with s_k = cell(T+2k+1,−1), for all k:
- cell(T+2k,−1)=1;
- cell(T+2k+1,1) = not s_k;
- cell(T+2k,−2) = cell(T+2k+1,−2) = not s_k;
- cell(T+2k,−3) = s_k and cell(T+2k+1,−3) = s_{k+1};
- s_k=0 ⇒ s_{k+1}=1 (golden mean);
- cell(T+2k+1,−4)=0.
Also: column −1 is not eventually periodic (Jen), so s is aperiodic. Enumeration shows no further cell in columns −5..−20 is forced, with or without the extra right-half realizability constraints.

claims.evidence:
Weak absorption in 100% of runs:
- 300+300+300 random starts of width 600;
- 60 starts at each of the widths 50, 200, 1000, 3000;
- 3000 structured starts per word (periodic, sparse, dense, perturbed);
- 100 starts for each of the 12 rotations.
Absorption time does not depend on width (mean about 50-90 steps, maximum about 800). The absorbed layer has fixed width (11, 10 and 8 columns) and stays stable for 12000 steps. Randomizing everything beyond the layer plus one column never breaks it. Contrast: period 2 (01) absorbs in about 3% of starts, and periods 3, 5, 6, 7 essentially never.

claims.statement:
Certified traps for period 4. These are explicit sets S_w of (phase, strip) states, closed under the strip relation for every value of column b+1, on which column 1 at the white phases follows a fixed periodic word:
- 0001: b=16, 109 states, output 001;
- 0011: b=16, 188 states, output 01;
- 0111: b=14, 188 states, output 0.
Consequence: if c = w^infinity from T, the true cells 1..b never form a state of S_w at the matching phase at any time >=T.

If the center column alternates from T (c(T+2k)=0 and c(T+2k+1)=1 for all k), then for all k:
- cell(T+2k+1,-1) = 1 and cell(T+2k,1) = not cell(T+2k,-1);
- cell(T+2k,-1) = 0 implies cell(T+2k+2,-1) = 1 (golden-mean shift);
- cell(T+2k,-2) = not cell(T+2k,-1) and cell(T+2k+1,-2) = not cell(T+2k+2,-1);
- cell(T+2k,-3) = cell(T+2k+1,-3) = cell(T+2k+2,-1);
- cell(T+2k,-4) = 0.
The other phase is the same statement started at T+1.

claims.proof:
Let Q = r+2 and let a_k be the start of the white run in period k. Shift t0 by one period so that a_k >= 1. The right edge cell(t,t) = 1 then gives a first black cell at column l_k + 1.
- **Fact Z.** During the white run, the leftmost black cell moves left by 1 per step, and column 1, once black, stays black.
- **Lock case, l <= r-8.** Column 1 is black at a+l .. a+r, so Lemma A gives cell(a+r+1,2) = 0. Then cell(a+r+1,1) = rule(1,1,.) = 0 and cell(a+r+2,1) = rule(1,0,0) = 1. So the next period has l' = 0, the lock self-sustains, and column 1 reads 1^(r+1) 0.
- **Case r-7 <= l <= r.** cell(a+r+1,1) = 0, so either cell(a+Q,1) = 1, or cell(a+r+1,2) = 1 and then cell(a+Q,1) = 0 and cell(a+Q,2) = 1. Either way l' <= 1.
- **Case l >= r+1.** At a+r the configuration is 0^z then 1, with z = l-r, and two explicit steps of rule 30 with driver 1 give:
  - z = 1 gives l' = 1;
  - z = 3 gives l' = 2;
  - z >= 4 gives l' = 1;
  - z = 2 gives column 1 = 0^(r+1) 1 in this period.
- **Conclusion (r >= 10, so 2 <= r-8).** Every case except z = 2 locks within two periods, and once locked it stays locked. So either l_k = r+2 for all k, and column 1 repeats 0^(r+1) 1, or column 1 eventually repeats 1^(r+1) 0. In both cases column 1 is eventually periodic, contradicting Jen.

verified_results.statement:
Realization and uniqueness (Condrey's Lemma 1, standard; Lean, kernel-checked by its author, adversarial review pending). For every right half R (positions ≥ 1) and every sequence c: N → {0,1}, exactly one configuration has right half R and centre column c. Its left half is obtained by driving the right half with c, setting col_{−1}(t) = c(t+1) ⊕ (c(t) ∨ col_1(t)), and reconstructing leftwards by left-permutivity. Consequences:
- every eventually periodic sequence is the centre column of some configuration with any prescribed right half;
- c is eventually w-periodic from T iff the true left half at T equals the realizer of (true right half at T, w^∞);
- any contradiction must use a property of the true left half that this realizer lacks, essentially its finiteness.
The round-1 claim that the realizer is always non-left-finite was unsupported and is withdrawn. Separately, for every n some configuration agrees with the seed at all p ≥ −n, matches c up to time n, and is white afterwards.

Period-2 rigid structure and the Q01 reduction (Lean; verified).
Normal form: if c(t+2) = c(t) for t ≥ T0, then for some T, c(T+2k) = 1 and c(T+2k+1) = 0 for all k. Write s_k = cell(T+2k+1,−1). Then:
- column −1 is black at every T+2k;
- cell(T+2k+1,1) = ¬s_k;
- s has no two consecutive zeros;
- cell(T+2k,−2) = cell(T+2k+1,−2) = ¬s_k;
- cell(T+2k,−3) = s_k and cell(T+2k+1,−3) = s_{k+1};
- column −4 is white at every T+2k+1.
At white times, write the column-1 pairs as A = 00, B = 01, C = 11 (10 is impossible). They never contain AA, BB, BC, CC, ABAB, ACBACA, CACBAC or ACACACB. Consequently consecutive white cells of column −1 are 4, 6, 8 or 10 steps apart, and after its first zero, s is a concatenation of 01, 011, 0111 and 01111 (this last step is checked by hand and by exhaustive word check, not in Lean).
Reduction: from T the left half is the zero-boundary left half. Q01 says no left start with a leftmost black cell keeps column −1 of the zero-boundary left half black at every even step. Q01 implies that c is not eventually 2-periodic, whatever the transient. Finiteness is essential: the infinite stripe, black exactly at odd depths, is a fixed point whose column −1 is always black. Equivalently, period 2 is excluded iff c(t) = c(t+1) for infinitely many t.

Complexity reformulations and window bounds.
(i) Standard (Morse–Hedlund): a binary sequence is eventually periodic iff any of the following holds, and these are all equivalent:
- its factor complexity has p(n) ≤ n for some n;
- p is bounded;
- for some n, no factor of length n is right-special;
- its ω-limit subshift is finite, in which case |X_c| equals the least period;
- some recurrent factor has eventually constant return words.
An eventual period (T,P) gives p(n) ≤ T+P.
(ii) Kernel-checked: if c(t+P) = c(t) for all t ≥ T with P > 0, then T+P ≥ 16359. Already in the project: no eventual period P ≤ 4096 with transient ≤ 8192.
(iii) Data only, not formal: T+P ≥ 9,999,957 from the 10^7-bit file; Wen's 10^9 bits exclude every P ≤ 2.5·10^8 with T ≤ 5·10^8.
Correction: in the 10^7-bit prefix, p_c(20) = 2^20 − 96, so full complexity holds only for n ≤ 19.

